  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe aim to find a sequence of non-negative numbers that minimizes the evaluation function: 2*n*max(convolve(sequence, sequence)[mode]) / (sum(sequence))^2. The key insight is that the optimal sequence for minimizing this ratio appears to be the uniform distribution across n elements, because it evenly distributes the energy, reducing the maximum value in the convolution product more effectively than sequences concentrated at specific points. We extend this by testing sequences with even energy distribution but potentially larger lengths, and we also employ a fallback initialization for larger sequences (n=2000) if the uniform approach does not quickly yield an improvement.\n\nMathematical specification\nFor a given sequence length n, define a uniform sequence of n elements as weight_uniform = ones(n) / n. The sum of this sequence is 1, and the maximum value in its convolution is 1/n. Thus, the evaluation function for this sequence is 2 * n * (1/n) / 1 = 2. Since the uniform sequence gives this value, it is a consistent candidate across all n. However, we can improve upon this by using a different sequence structure, such as the golden distribution, which has better theoretical performance.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Read the initial sequence from the parent variable (height_sequence_1) if available. Otherwise, use fallback initialization.\n2. Convert the parent sequence to a list of float values.\n3. Run a full optimization pipeline for each sequence length n in the list of targeting n values (uniform_distribute, distributed_golden):\n   - The distribution is created by sparse placement: elements are at two separate points in the sequence.\n   - This is justified because earlier experiments with uniform distribution gave an objective of 2, and we want to see if we can achieve better.\n4. Use mirrordescent optimization (as a direct inspiration from the previous code) but adapted to avoid using the legacy mirror_descent_optimize and lp_refine functions. Instead, define new functions that use a smoothed version of the objective.\n5. For each candidate from the distribution, evaluate the sequence and keep track of the best one found.\n6. Terminate the search after a time budget of 1500 seconds.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- We target n values from a pre-defined list: [50,100,200,300,400,500,700,1000,1500,2000].\n- The golden distribution is used to systematically pick two points in the sequence.\n- The initializations include uniform distribution and the two-point (golden-like) distribution.\n- We allow the same n as the parent and our own fallback sequences.\n- The number of initializations per n is fixed to 2.\n\nCompute budget and robustness\n- Allocate 1500 seconds for the search.\n- For each n, run the optimization until time is exhausted or the candidate improves.\n- Return the best candidate found initially and safeguard the fallback.\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Only return a sequence after validating that the evaluation function gives a finite value and the sequence contains only non-negative numbers.\n- Re-compute the evaluation function on the best candidate to ensure fairness.\n\nNote: This approach is designed to offer a known, theoretically-bound solution directly without relying on ad-hoc optimization methods. The fallback initialization is minimal to prevent termination due to unavailable candidates.",
